(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Approximately how long is the small intestine, occupying much of the abdominal cavity?","choices":["About 1.5 meters (5 feet)","About 6 meters (20 feet)","About 12 meters (40 feet)","About 0.5 meters"],"answer":1,"exp":"The small intestine is a thin-walled coiled tube about 6 meters (20 feet) long, occupying much of the abdominal cavity between the stomach and the large intestine. — Slide 60 (Small Intestine)"},{"q":"The lateral walls of the oral cavity proper are formed by the cheeks, which contain which muscle?","choices":["Buccinator","Masseter","Orbicularis oris","Mylohyoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cheeks, which form the lateral walls of the oral cavity proper, contain the buccinator muscle; the lips are formed by orbicularis oris. — Slide 26 (Oral Cavity)"},{"q":"The hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments together form which peritoneal structure?","choices":["The falciform ligament","The greater omentum","The lesser omentum","The mesentery proper"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lesser omentum is composed of the hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments, running from the porta hepatis of the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the duodenum. — Slide 21 (Peritoneal Reflections)"},{"q":"The colon lining is notable for an abundance of which cells that provide lubrication?","choices":["Chief cells","Parietal cells","Goblet cells","Paneth cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"The colon has an abundance of goblet cells that secrete mucus for lubrication; the colon also lacks villi and does not produce digestive enzymes. — Slide 88 (Characteristics of the Colon)"},{"q":"The pyloric sphincter controls gastric emptying into which structure?","choices":["The duodenum","The jejunum","The esophagus","The cecum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pyloric sphincter regulates the emptying of stomach contents (chyme) into the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine. — Slides 54, 61 (Stomach / Small Intestine)"},{"q":"The splenic artery characteristically follows which course relative to the pancreas?","choices":["Within the substance of the mesentery proper","Through the head and uncinate process of the pancreas","Posterior to the aorta and inferior vena cava","Along the superior margin of the body and tail"],"answer":3,"exp":"The splenic artery, a branch of the celiac trunk, runs along the superior margin of the body and tail of the pancreas en route to the spleen. — Slides 60, 137 (Splenic Artery)"},{"q":"The duodenum receives bile and pancreatic secretions at which structure on its descending part?","choices":["The ileocecal valve junction","The pyloric sphincter opening","The greater and lesser papillae","The duodenal cap (bulb) region"],"answer":2,"exp":"The descending (second) part of the duodenum bears the greater and lesser duodenal papillae, where the bile duct and pancreatic duct(s) empty their secretions. — Slide 66 (Duodenum)"},{"q":"The appendix is dominated by which tissue, making it a lymphoid organ?","choices":["Adipose fat tissue","Mucous goblet cells","Smooth muscle fibers","Lymphoid nodules"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vermiform appendix is dominated by lymphoid nodules, which is why it is classified as a lymphoid organ; it is fixed to the cecum by the mesoappendix. — Slides 85, 87 (The Appendix)"},{"q":"The head of the pancreas is bounded inferiorly by which structure?","choices":["The common bile duct","The splenic artery","The left colic flexure","The ligament of Treitz"],"answer":0,"exp":"The head of the pancreas is bounded superiorly by the gastroduodenal artery and inferiorly by the common bile duct. — Slide 76 (Head of pancreas)"},{"q":"The lesser omentum attaches to the liver at which region?","choices":["The falciform ligament","The bare area","The porta hepatis","The right triangular ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lesser omentum attaches to the liver surrounding the porta hepatis and extends to the caudate and left lobes. — Slides 117–118 (Lesser Omentum)"},{"q":"The superior mesenteric vein passes in which relationship to the pancreas before joining the portal vein?","choices":["Directly through the liver parenchyma itself","Anterior to the body of the stomach wall","Within the leaves of the falciform ligament","Deep (posterior) to the neck of the pancreas"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior mesenteric vein passes deep (posterior) to the neck of the pancreas, where it merges with the splenic vein to form the portal vein. — Slides 63, 135 (Mesenteric Veins)"},{"q":"The gallbladder concentrates bile, which is produced by which organ?","choices":["The duodenum","The pancreas","The spleen","The liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bile is produced by the liver's hepatocytes and is stored and concentrated in the gallbladder until needed for digestion. — Slides 104, 126 (Liver / Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The left gastric artery is a direct branch of which vessel?","choices":["The superior mesenteric artery","The celiac trunk","The common hepatic artery","The splenic artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The left gastric artery is one of the three direct branches of the celiac trunk, along with the splenic and common hepatic arteries. — Slide 139 (Arterial Supply)"},{"q":"The IVC receives the azygos communication and pierces the diaphragm at which level?","choices":["T8 (caval hiatus)","T10 (esophageal hiatus)","T12 (aortic hiatus)","L1"],"answer":0,"exp":"The IVC passes through the diaphragm's caval hiatus at the T8 vertebral level. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"Which nodal group would drain hindgut structures such as the descending and sigmoid colon?","choices":["Inferior mesenteric nodes","Celiac nodes","Superior mesenteric nodes","Hepatic nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior mesenteric nodes drain hindgut derivatives such as the descending and sigmoid colon, following the territory of the inferior mesenteric artery. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"The thymus, an endocrine organ that atrophies in adulthood, secretes which hormones?","choices":["Mineralocorticoids","Thyroxines","Catecholamines","Thymosins"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thymus secretes thymosins, which support T-lymphocyte maturation, and undergoes involution (atrophy) with age into adulthood. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Thymus)"},{"q":"The anterior pituitary hormones such as ACTH and TSH function primarily to:","choices":["Filter blood plasma within the nephron","Control the activity of other endocrine organs","Store and mobilize calcium from bone reserves","Produce and secrete bile from the liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hormones from the anterior pituitary (pars distalis), such as ACTH and TSH, primarily act to control the activity of other endocrine organs (the adrenal cortex and thyroid, respectively). — Slides 5, 11 (Pituitary hormones)"},{"q":"The exocrine (acinar) cells of the pancreas are distinct from the islet cells in that they:","choices":["Secrete insulin directly into the blood","Secrete digestive enzymes rather than hormones","Store colloid within follicular lumens","Are chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The exocrine acinar cells of the pancreas secrete digestive enzymes rather than hormones, distinguishing them from the hormone-secreting endocrine islet cells. — Slide 52 (Anatomy of the Pancreas)"},{"q":"The follicular epithelium of an actively secreting thyroid follicle tends to be:","choices":["Stratified squamous in multiple layers","Flattened and squamous, resting state","Taller (columnar-like) rather than flat","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Actively secreting thyroid follicles have taller ('high,' columnar-like) follicular epithelium, whereas inactive follicles have flatter epithelium. — Slide 30 (Histology of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The tail of the pancreas, an endocrine-containing organ, reaches which structure?","choices":["The porta hepatis region","The hilum of the spleen","The right kidney's hilum","The gallbladder fossa area"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tail of the pancreas extends to reach the hilum of the spleen. — Slide 56 (Pancreas relationships)"},{"q":"The lateral surface of the thyroid gland is related to the sternomastoid and which muscle group?","choices":["Infrahyoid (strap) muscles","Suprahyoid neck muscles","Anterior scalene muscles","Pharyngeal constrictor muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lateral surface of the thyroid gland relates to the sternocleidomastoid and the infrahyoid (strap) muscles (except thyrohyoid). — Slide 28 (Thyroid lateral surface)"},{"q":"Calcitonin and PTH exemplify which type of hormonal control over blood calcium?","choices":["Synergistic (cooperative) control","Antagonistic (opposing) control","Permissive (enabling) hormone control","Feedforward (anticipatory) control"],"answer":1,"exp":"PTH (which raises blood Ca2+) and calcitonin (which lowers blood Ca2+) are antagonistic hormones that work in opposition to regulate blood calcium levels. — Slides 31, 44 (calcium regulation)"},{"q":"The accessory glands of the reproductive system are defined by their role in:","choices":["Secreting fluids","Producing gametes","Forming the perineum","Housing the testes"],"answer":0,"exp":"The accessory glands of the reproductive system (such as the seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands) are defined by their role in secreting fluids that support the gametes. — Slide 4 (Organization of the Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The external genitalia of the reproductive system are described as:","choices":["Gamete-producing organs","Perineal structures","Ducts for gamete storage","Fluid-secreting glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"The external genitalia are described as perineal structures, distinguishing them from the internal gonads, ducts, and accessory glands. — Slide 4 (Organization of the Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The cremaster's role in thermoregulation is to:","choices":["Stimulate Leydig cells to produce testosterone","Wrinkle and thicken the scrotal skin surface","Cool the testes through active sweat secretion","Elevate the testes toward the body when cold"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cremaster muscle contracts to elevate (draw) the testes toward the warm body when the environment is cold, helping maintain optimal testicular temperature. — Slide 19 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"Both the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus are what type of muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Involuntary smooth muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus are perineal skeletal (voluntary) muscles that produce the forceful contractions involved in ejaculation. — Slide 67 (ejaculation)"},{"q":"The scrotum receives contributions from the external pudendal artery, a branch of the:","choices":["Aorta","Internal iliac artery","External iliac artery","Femoral artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external pudendal artery, which helps supply the scrotum, arises as a branch of the femoral artery. — Slide 21 (scrotal blood supply)"},{"q":"The ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves are notable because they:","choices":["Control the detrusor muscle of the urinary bladder","Provide the sole motor innervation to the diaphragm","Supply autonomic innervation directly to the kidney","Supply the scrotum and travel with the spermatic cord"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ilioinguinal nerve and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supply the scrotum, with the genitofemoral nerve's genital branch traveling within the spermatic cord. — Slides 14, 22 (spermatic cord / scrotal nerves)"},{"q":"Which portion of the epididymis becomes the ductus deferens?","choices":["The tail","The head","The body","The efferent ductules"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the tail of the epididymis, the coiled duct uncoils and becomes continuous with the single ductus (vas) deferens. — Slide 42 (The Epididymis)"},{"q":"After the ejaculatory duct, semen next enters which portion of the urethra?","choices":["The spongy urethra","The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra","The external urethral orifice"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ejaculatory duct empties directly into the prostatic urethra, the first (most proximal) segment of the male urethra. — Slides 48, 51 (Ductus Deferens / Urethra)"},{"q":"The bulbo-urethral glands empty their secretions into which urethral segment?","choices":["The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra","The spongy urethra","The ejaculatory duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands empty their secretions into the spongy (penile) urethra, just distal to the membranous urethra. — Slide 57 (Bulbo-urethral Glands)"},{"q":"The ovary is homologous to which male structure?","choices":["The scrotum","The prostate","The penis","The testis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ovaries are embryologically homologous to the testes, both developing from the same indifferent gonadal tissue. — Slide 26 (Ovaries)"},{"q":"The uterine (fallopian) tubes are homologous to which male structure?","choices":["The scrotal raphe structure","The prostate gland tissue","The vas (ductus) deferens","The glans of the penis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine (fallopian) tubes, derived from the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts, are homologous to the vas (ductus) deferens, which derives from the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct system in males. — Slide 50 (Uterine Tubes)"},{"q":"The lateral boundaries of the perineum are formed by the:","choices":["Ischial tuberosities","Anterior pubic rami","Sacroiliac joint margins","Femoral head prominences"],"answer":0,"exp":"The perineum's lateral boundaries are formed by the ischial tuberosities of the ossa coxae. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The medial aspects of the thighs and the gluteal folds serve as which type of perineal reference?","choices":["Vascular landmarks of the pelvic floor","Deep muscular boundaries of the pelvis","Surface boundaries of the female perineum","Lymphatic drainage landmarks nearby"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial aspects of the thighs and the gluteal folds serve as surface (external) boundaries of the female perineum. — Slide 8 (Boundaries of Female Perineum)"},{"q":"The mesovarium is a subdivision of which larger peritoneal structure?","choices":["The broad ligament","The round ligament","The suspensory ligament","The uterosacral ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mesovarium is a double fold of peritoneum that is a subdivision of the broad ligament, attaching the ovary to its posterior surface. — Slides 14, 28 (Peritoneal Folds)"},{"q":"The pelvic diaphragm functions to seal which opening?","choices":["The inferior opening of the bony pelvis","The superior inlet of the bony pelvis","The obturator foramen on each side","The greater sciatic foramen opening"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm seals the inferior opening of the bony pelvis, forming the muscular floor that supports the pelvic organs. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The ovarian artery reaches the ovary after entering which ligament?","choices":["The cardinal ligament","The round ligament","The uterosacral ligament","The suspensory ligament"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ovarian artery enters the suspensory ligament of the ovary and then branches through the mesovarium to reach the ovary. — Slide 27 (Vasculature of Ovaries)"},{"q":"Inferior lymphatic drainage of the vagina includes which superficial node group?","choices":["Axillary lymph node chain","Superficial inguinal nodes","Popliteal lymph node group","Submental lymph node chain"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior vagina drains in part to the superficial inguinal nodes, in addition to sacral and common iliac nodes for its upper portions. — Slide 64 (Lymphatic Drainage of Vagina)"},{"q":"The vagina lies posterior to which organ and anterior to the rectum?","choices":["The uterus","The urethra","The bladder fundus","The ovary"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vagina lies posterior to the urethra and anterior to the rectum, forming a key part of the pelvic floor's anteroposterior arrangement. — Slide 57 (Vagina)"},{"q":"The labia majora bear which features distinguishing them histologically from the labia minora?","choices":["Hair, sweat, and sebaceous glands","A core of erectile tissue only","Simple ciliated columnar epithelium","Colloid-filled follicles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The labia majora have pigmented outer skin bearing hair, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands, unlike the labia minora, which lack hair and fat. — Slides 73–74 (Labia)"},{"q":"Peritoneum folding around the pelvic organs creates two dead-end recesses called:","choices":["The greater and lesser peritoneal sacs","The vesicouterine and rectouterine pouches","The right and left paracolic gutters","The mesovarium and mesosalpinx folds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Peritoneum folding around the female pelvic organs creates the anterior vesicouterine pouch (between bladder and uterus) and the posterior rectouterine pouch (between uterus and rectum). — Slide 12 (Anatomy of the Female Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The kidneys are considered the primary organs of the urinary system because they:","choices":["Transport urine","Store urine","Filter waste and form urine","Provide voluntary control of urination"],"answer":2,"exp":"The kidneys are the primary organs of the urinary system because they filter waste from the blood and form urine; the rest of the system transports and stores it. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"Multiple small prostatic ducts enter which urethral region during sexual stimulation?","choices":["The penile urethra","The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra","The external urethral orifice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Multiple small prostatic ducts open into the prostatic urethra, the segment of urethra that passes through the prostate gland. — Slide 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The base of each renal pyramid abuts which region, while the apex points inward?","choices":["The renal pelvis","The cortex","The hilum","The minor calyx"],"answer":1,"exp":"The base of each renal pyramid abuts the renal cortex, while its apex (the renal papilla) points inward toward a minor calyx. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The layer that anchors the kidney to the posterior body wall is the:","choices":["Paranephric fat","Fibrous capsule","Perinephric fat","Renal fascia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The renal fascia, a layer of dense irregular connective tissue, anchors the kidney and its fat to the posterior abdominal wall and peritoneum. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The renal vein ultimately drains into which vessel?","choices":["Azygos venous system","Abdominal aorta trunk","Hepatic portal vein","Inferior vena cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"Venous return from the kidney proceeds through the renal vein directly into the inferior vena cava. — Slides 6, 18 (Kidney Location / Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Approximately what fraction of nephrons are the cortical type?","choices":["15%","85%","50%","25%"],"answer":1,"exp":"About 85% of nephrons are the cortical type, characterized by short loops of Henle and peritubular capillaries; the remaining ~15% are juxtamedullary. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"A common site for kidney stones to lodge is the ureteropelvic junction because of:","choices":["The gonadal artery crossing the ureter at that level","A true anatomic sphincter present at that junction","The dense presence of transitional epithelium there","The transition in ductal lumen size from the renal pelvis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ureteropelvic junction is a common obstruction site because of the transition in lumen size from the wider renal pelvis to the narrower ureter, which predisposes stones to lodge there. — Slide 47 (Zones Related to the Ureters)"},{"q":"The basement membrane of the glomerulus restricts filtration of albumin because it contains:","choices":["A negatively charged proteoglycan gel","A layer of ciliated columnar epithelial cells","Dense chromaffin secretory granules","A layer of stored thyroglobulin colloid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The glomerular basement membrane contains a negatively charged proteoglycan gel that repels large, negatively charged molecules such as albumin, restricting their filtration. — Slide 34 (Filtration Membranes)"}]};
